---
// Project detail — one page per project in the portfolio, the screen the
// Projects index and the Recently viewed row have been linking at.
//
// A PROJECT IS A HUB, AND WHAT LEADS IT IS WHAT IS TRACKED. Of everything on a
// project record, only some of it has a history worth reading: what was
// promised against what has been delivered, spent, and reached. That is a
// SERIES — its account over time is the point — and it is what the main column
// leads with, in the order a reader judges delivery: measures (what was built),
// expenditures (what it cost), funding sources (who paid for it), milestones
// (when it happened). Everything else on the record — lead organization,
// program, county, the narrative description — is a SCALAR: a fact that can be
// corrected but has no interesting history of its own. That is the index card,
// and ALL of it belongs in the rail: present, because a reader needs it to place
// the numbers, but not prominent, because it is not what the page exists to
// answer. The description is the one that has tested that rule — it spent a
// release under the page title on the argument that "what IS this project" comes
// before any number — and it is back on the index card, as the last row of
// Key facts, because the argument was for reading ORDER and the position it bought
// was PROMINENCE.
//
// FUNDING SOURCES IS THE OTHER TEST, and it went the other way. It is a set of
// scalars by the definition above — each funder's commitment is a fact with no
// history — so it spent a release in the rail, and came back. Two things it got
// wrong there: a four-column table does not fit 22rem (it overflowed and the
// rail answered with a horizontal scrollbar), and separating "who committed the
// budget" from "what has been spent of it" put two halves of the same question
// in different columns a screen apart. The lesson is that the rail's test is not
// only "is this a scalar" — it is also "is this SHORT and NARROW", and a table
// of funders fails both.
//
// THE INDEX CARD IS A PINNED RAIL, and that is the one thing on this page that
// is not simply "present, not prominent". The rail holds the fold for the entire
// page rather than scrolling away with the first screen, because the numbers it
// carries are what the numbers below are READ AGAINST. An expenditure table two
// screens down means nothing without the estimated total cost, and a reader
// should not have to scroll back for it. Present-not-prominent is about
// emphasis; this is about availability, and they are not the same constraint.
//
// THE MAP IS NEITHER A SERIES NOR A SCALAR, and it is the third section to test
// the rule. It is real workflow surface (its own Add-area affordance), it answers
// WHERE, and it is the only thing on this page that gets better the more room it
// has — so it keeps the full width of the column and no card. It comes LAST all
// the same. Leading, it put zero pixels of tracked data above the fold, which
// breaks the first thing this page owes a reader; last, it is what it always was,
// the context you drop to once you have read the numbers. Room and position are
// different currencies, and this section gets the first without the second.
//
// WHO IT IS FOR. Not only the sponsor. An executive director checking whether
// their organization's project is on track, and a member of the public who
// found it from the index, land on the same page and both want the same
// question answered first: is this project doing what it said it would.
//
// IT IS ALSO A WORKFLOW ENTRY POINT — but not through an edit BUTTON. Every
// collection section carries an "Add" affordance, and every scalar edits where
// it sits. The header carries no EDIT action: an "Edit project" button stood
// there for a release and was deleted, because a site that is always editable
// and always saved has no mode for it to enter (see the note in the markup, and
// the brief's §3). What the header DOES carry is the record-level actions that
// touch nothing on the record — Share, Fact sheet, History — see
// firma2-project-actions for why those three are a different kind of thing
// from the button that was deleted. The Add affordances are inert in this prototype — they
// render, focus, and take a click, and nothing happens. They are deliberately
// NOT `disabled`: a greyed-out control tells a reviewer the action is
// unavailable to them, which is a different and false claim than "not wired
// yet". Expenditures is the one section whose action is not an "Add": it says
// "Record spend", because a year of money is FILED against a period as that
// period closes, not appended to a list — and unlike the Add affordances it is
// live, writing to a browser-local store the same way Record work does. See
// that component's own header.
//
// ROUTE. getStaticPaths builds one page per project from `projects`, keyed by
// projectSlug — the SAME function projectHref() uses, so a link in the index or
// the recently-viewed row can never point at a slug this route did not
// generate. firma2-projects.ts throws at build on a slug collision, which is
// what keeps that guarantee honest as projects are added.
//
// ZERO PAGE CSS. Every section owns its own markup and styles; this file is the
// spine and the manifest, nothing else.
import AppLayout from '../../../layouts/AppLayout.astro';
import Firma2PageHeader from '../../../components/shared/firma2-page-header.astro';
import EsaPill from '@esa/ecology/esa-pill.astro';
import Firma2RecordRail from '../../../components/shared/firma2-record-rail.astro';
import Firma2ProjectRail from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-rail.astro';
import Firma2ProjectMap from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-map.astro';
import Firma2ProjectMeasures from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-measures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectExpenditures from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-expenditures.astro';
import Firma2ProjectFunding from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-funding.astro';
import Firma2ProjectTimeline from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-timeline.astro';
import Firma2ProjectGallery from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-gallery.astro';
import Firma2ProjectActions from '../../../components/projects/firma2-project-actions.astro';
import { projects, projectSlug, STAGE_TONE } from '../../../data/firma2-projects';
import type { Project } from '../../../data/firma2-projects';
import { getProjectDetail } from '../../../data/firma2-project-detail';
import { withBase } from '../../../lib/base';

export function getStaticPaths() {
  return projects.map((project) => ({
    params: { project: projectSlug(project) },
    props: { project },
  }));
}

interface Props {
  project: Project;
}

const { project } = Astro.props;
const detail = getProjectDetail(project);
---
<!-- manifest:
  layout: sidebar[data-side=end] > [ rail · stack(xl) ]
  sections:
    - rail (100dvh, sticky)  -> firma2-record-rail
      - rail tabs               -> firma2-project-rail   (composes facts, contacts, comments)
    - page header            -> firma2-page-header      (band)
      - stage                   -> esa-pill
      - record actions          -> firma2-project-actions   (share, fact sheet, change-history sheet)
    - performance measures   -> firma2-project-measures
    - expenditures           -> firma2-project-expenditures
    - funding sources        -> firma2-project-funding
    - milestones             -> firma2-project-timeline
    - photos                 -> firma2-project-gallery
    - work areas map         -> firma2-project-map      (full column, to the fold)
  note: the rail is the SCALAR index card and holds the fold for the whole page,
        and its three tabs are peers — what this project IS, who to call about
        it, what has been said about it — so all three stay one click from the
        fold instead of one, two and three screens down it;
        the rail carries two controls of its own — a collapse button at its head
        and a bcn-edge-handle on its leading edge that drags the track wider — so
        the width below is a default rather than a verdict;
        the main column leads with TRACKED data — what was built, what it cost,
        who paid for it, when it happened — and closes with the map, which is
        CONTEXT for those numbers rather than an answer to the page's question.
-->
<AppLayout title={`${project.projectName} — ProjectFirma 2.0`} active="projects">
  <!-- TWO COLUMNS FOR THE WHOLE PAGE, not for one section of it. `.sidebar` is
       the lego for a fixed rail beside a fluid main, and this is the outermost
       element on the page precisely so the rail is beside EVERYTHING — the
       header band included. That is the difference between a rail and a panel:
       the record does not sit next to the map, it sits next to the page.

       ITS FIRST DOM CHILD IS ALWAYS THE ASIDE, and data-side="end" orders it to
       the trailing edge, so the rail renders on the right while staying first in
       the source. Reversing the DOM to match the eye would mean fighting the
       primitive, which reads its aside off :first-child.

       THE PRIMITIVE'S OWN --gap, and it was briefly set to 0. That was solving
       the right problem the wrong way: the map and the rail are both meant to
       reach their edges, so a gutter between them would have been a stripe of
       page background running the full height of the screen. Zeroing the gap
       closed it — and took the gutter away from everything ELSE in the column
       too, so all four cards below the map butted straight into the rail with no
       margin at all.

       The gap belongs here; the map is the exception and it already handles
       itself. It bleeds --spacing-500 past its column on both edges, which is
       exactly this gap, so it crosses the gutter and meets the rail while the
       cards below it stop short of it by one step. One value, two behaviours,
       and neither is a special case bolted onto the layout. The header band does
       the same thing for the same reason.

       --sidebar-width: 25rem IS A TRACK WIDTH, and it is 3rem wider than the
       22rem the cards inside it want because the rail spends --spacing-500 of
       padding on each side out of that track. The bleed adds nothing to it — a
       negative margin lets a box overflow its track, it does not widen it — so
       the rail reaches the window's edge at the same 25rem it was given.
       firma2-record-rail states the same arithmetic where it is applied.

       --sidebar-content-min AS A LENGTH, the same choice and the same reason as
       the measure setup page's rail: the 60% default is a fraction of a container
       whose width already depends on the app nav's collapse state, so the wrap
       point moves for a reason that has nothing to do with whether the content
       still fits. 40rem is a floor for the main column stated in the same units
       the content is measured in. The rail unpins itself once that wrap
       happens — see its own media query, and the note there about why a viewport
       query is standing in for a container one. -->
  <div
    class="sidebar"
    data-side="end"
    style="--sidebar-width: 25rem; --sidebar-content-min: 40rem"
  >
    <!-- THE RECORD, PINNED FOR AS LONG AS THE PAGE LASTS. Key facts is SCALARS —
         correctable facts with no history worth reading — and it is here rather
         than in the main column for the reason it always was: a reader needs
         these to place the numbers, and they are not what the page exists to
         answer. What is new is that they no longer scroll away from the numbers
         they are placing. The expenditure table two screens down is read against
         the estimated total cost in this rail, and until it was pinned that
         number was a screen and a half behind.

         THREE TABS, NOT THREE STACKED SECTIONS, and firma2-project-rail owns
         that argument: a pinned column exists so a reader does not have to
         scroll, and stacking contacts and comments under the facts would put
         them below the fold again — spending the guarantee the pinning bought.
         Tabbed, each of the three is one click away rather than one, two and
         three screens down. What tabs cost is that only one is visible at a
         time, which is affordable precisely because the three are PEERS: a
         reader arrives wanting one of them and knows which.

         THE TEST THE RAIL APPLIES IS UNCHANGED. Funding sources was in here and
         has gone back to the main column — see its own placement note below. A
         rail row is a FACT, short enough to read at a glance and narrow enough
         to live at 22rem, and tabs do not relax that: they multiply the column,
         they do not widen it. A four-column table of funders would fail in a
         tab exactly as it failed in the stack. -->
    <Firma2RecordRail label="project details">
      <Firma2ProjectRail detail={detail} />
    </Firma2RecordRail>

  <!-- `xl`, not the 2xl this stack used to carry: it is the gap between the
       header band and the first section, and xl is the rhythm the cards
       already keep between themselves — so the page reads as one interval
       throughout instead of a wider one at the top.

       THE BAND AND THE MAP ARE DIRECT CHILDREN because both bleed past this
       stack's inline edges with a negative margin, which only reaches the
       pane's padding from a child of something full-width.

       THE BAND'S OWN TRAILING BLEED IS LEFT ALONE, and there is a hook for
       closing it (--firma2-band-bleed-end) that this page briefly used. It was
       needed while the sidebar's gap was 0: the band bleeds a step past its
       column, the column ended flush against the rail, so the bleed
       painted a 24px notch of band background over the rail's top corner. With
       the gap restored the bleed lands exactly IN the gutter and stops on the
       rail's leading edge, which is where it should have been stopping all
       along. Nothing overlaps, and the band still reads as the top of the page
       rather than a card floating in it. -->
  <div class="stack" data-gap="xl">
    <!-- Two crumbs, not three. "Home > Projects > <name>" would spend a step on a
         route this app's sidenav already owns; the one thing a reader needs from
         here is the way back to the list they came from. The last crumb takes no
         href and esa-breadcrumbs marks it aria-current="page". -->
    <!-- THE TITLE IS A FIELD, not a screen name. Every other stored value on this
         record can be corrected where it is read — lead organization, program,
         county, timeline, cost, the description — and the project's NAME is the
         one that was still read-only, which made it the odd field out on its own
         page. `editableTitle` hands it the same choreography: hover or focus the
         h1, the pencil appears beside it, Enter saves and Escape abandons. The
         breadcrumb below repeats the name and the header keeps it in step. -->
    <Firma2PageHeader
      title={project.projectName}
      editableTitle={{ field: 'projectName', label: 'Project name' }}
      crumbs={[
        { label: 'Projects', href: withBase('/prototypes/projects') },
        { label: project.projectName },
      ]}
    >
      <!-- The stage pill sits UNDER the title, in firma2-page-header's status
           slot. It spent a release in the actions cluster, because that was the
           only seat esa-page-header offered beside the title — and it put the
           project's state on the far trailing edge of a bar that runs the width
           of the content column, among controls, when it is not a control: no
           href, no handler, not focusable. Under the name it is read in the same
           glance as the name, which is the order the record is asked about
           anyway — what it is, then where it has got to.

           The visually-hidden "Stage" is load-bearing. A pill reading
           "Implementation" beside a project name is unambiguous to a sighted
           reader only because of where it sits; announced on its own it could
           as easily be a project type or a program. This is the label that used
           to sit in the Key facts panel, which is why the panel no longer repeats
           the pill. -->
      <span slot="status" class="visually-hidden">Stage</span>
      <EsaPill
        slot="status"
        label={project.stage}
        variant={STAGE_TONE[project.stage]}
        size="md"
        removable={false}
      />
      <!-- THERE IS STILL NO "EDIT PROJECT" BUTTON, and its absence is still
           the point. One stood here — primary, solid brand green, and inert —
           and it contradicted the stance the whole site takes: always
           editable, always saved, no view mode and no edit mode (see the
           brief, §3). Every field on this page already edits where it sits; a
           button offering to "edit the project" describes a mode this app
           does not have.

           WHAT STANDS IN THE BAND INSTEAD PASSES A DIFFERENT TEST: Share,
           Fact sheet and History touch nothing on the record. They hand the
           page to someone else, take it away as a file, or read what
           happened to it — actions on the WHOLE record with no section to
           belong to. They ride the TOOLBAR slot — the breadcrumb row, not
           the title's bar — because they are chrome, not workflow: the crumb
           strip is the page's frame (the way out on one edge, the things you
           can do with the page on the other), and the title stays the
           record's alone (user-directed 2026-08-25; one release at md beside
           the title first). The full argument, and the live/inert split, is
           firma2-project-actions' own header. -->
      <Firma2ProjectActions slot="toolbar" audit={detail.audit} />
    </Firma2PageHeader>

    <div class="stack" data-gap="xl">
      <Firma2ProjectMeasures measures={detail.measures} projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)} />
      <Firma2ProjectExpenditures
        expenditures={detail.expenditures}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
        estimatedAnnualOperatingCost={detail.project.estimatedAnnualOperatingCost}
        projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)}
      />
      <!-- DIRECTLY AFTER EXPENDITURES, and that placement is the argument for
           moving it out of the rail rather than just the place it fits. The
           section above says what the project is budgeted to cost and what it
           has SPENT against that; this one says who put the budget up. They are
           two halves of the money on
           this project, and reading them back to back is how "$359,040 of
           $528,000" becomes a fact about three named funders instead of two
           numbers. In the rail they were a screen apart and in different
           columns.

           It also gets its width back. Four columns — two of them long
           organization names, two non-wrapping figures — had 22rem in the rail
           and overflowed it; here they have the main column, which is what the
           table was designed against. -->
      <Firma2ProjectFunding
        funding={detail.funding}
        estimatedTotalCost={detail.project.estimatedTotalCost}
        projectSlug={projectSlug(detail.project)}
      />
      <Firma2ProjectTimeline milestones={detail.milestones} />

      <!-- AFTER THE TRACKED DATA, BEFORE THE MAP — photos are the same
           currency as the map: CONTEXT for the numbers, not an answer to the
           page's question, so they follow everything tracked. They come
           BEFORE the map for the reason the map is last: the map is the
           surface the column lands on and holds to the fold, and a card
           after it would dangle below that landing. A card, unlike the map,
           because a photo grid does not get better with every pixel — three
           tiles abreast at caption-readable size is its natural extent. -->
      <Firma2ProjectGallery photos={detail.photos} />

      <!-- LAST, AND FULL WIDTH. Those are two separate decisions and this section
           has now had every combination of them.

           LAST, because the page owes ONE question an answer above the fold — "is
           this project doing what it said it would" — and the map does not answer
           it. It spent a release leading the column, on the reasoning that a
           restoration project is a place before it is a budget. That reasoning is
           still true and it is an argument about what a project IS, not about
           what this page is for: reading order, not prominence. Leading, the map
           put ZERO pixels of tracked data above the fold — every number that
           answers the page's question began after a scroll. So it comes last,
           where it has always belonged: CONTEXT for the numbers above it, the
           thing you drop to when you want to see where all of this happened.

           This is the same mistake the description made and the same correction —
           see the module header. Twice now, a section has argued for reading
           ORDER and been given PROMINENCE, which is not the same currency.

           FULL WIDTH, unchanged, because that decision was never the problem. The
           map is the only section here that gets better with every pixel, and the
           only one that is a surface rather than a block of text — so it keeps the
           whole column, edge to edge, and keeps no card. Being last does not make
           it small; it makes it the screen you arrive at rather than the one you
           scroll past. Halving its width was considered and costs every OTHER
           section its width permanently to buy one screen. -->
      <Firma2ProjectMap detail={detail} />
    </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</AppLayout>
